4},"cost_structure":{"cost_of_sales_percent_revenue":44.65,"overheads_percent_revenue":39.1,"staff_costs_percent_revenue":0.38,"director_costs_percent_revenue":17.19,"top_5_overheads":[{"category":"Directors Pension","value":64000,"percent_of_overheads":36.74},{"category":"Other Overheads (incl. Loss on Disposal)","value":33974,"percent_of_overheads":19.5},{"category":"Rent & Rates","value":15077,"percent_of_overheads":8.65},{"category":"Motor Expenses","value":13046,"percent_of_overheads":7.49},{"category":"Directors Salaries","value":12576,"percent_of_overheads":7.22}],"cost_base_classification":"mixed"},"earnings_quality":{"net_profit_tracks_operating_profit":true,"exceptional_items_identified":["Loss on disposal of tangible fixed assets: £32,145 (2025)","Significant discretionary Director Pension contributions: £64,000 (2025)","One-off van purchase: £28,000 (2023)"],"tax_rate_consistent":false,"ebitda_latest":81554,"ebitda_trend":"stable","limitations":["Management accounts for 2026 lack cost data","Depreciation not consistently split in all source documents"]},"debt_service":{"latest_interest_coverage":null,"interest_coverage_trend":"stable","additional_debt_capacity_assessment":"Excellent. The company is currently debt-free with strong cash reserves and consistent EBITDA, providing significant headroom for acquisition leverage."},"key_findings":["Consistent organic revenue growth with a 4-year CAGR of 6.4%.","Significant gross margin expansion from 48.8% to 55.4% due to reduced reliance on subcontractors.","Operating margins are compressed by high discretionary spending, specifically director pensions and salaries.","Exceptional liquidity maintained throughout the period, despite high dividend payouts.","Strategic shift from variable subcontractor costs to fixed direct labour is evident in the P&L.","Statutory profitability is significantly understated due to owner-managed 'add-backs'.","The 2025 net asset erosion is a result of dividends exceeding net profit, not operational failure."],"red_flags":["Dividends exceeded net profit in 2023 and 2025, leading to capital erosion.","Significant loss on asset disposal (£32k) in 2025 requires further investigation.","Management accounts for early 2026 show 100% margins, indicating incomplete bookkeeping."],"narrative":"Greener Estates presents a profile of a stable, maturing service business with high earnings quality once discretionary owner-managed costs are normalised. Revenue has grown consistently at approximately 6% per annum, which is healthy for the facilities management sector. A key positive trend is the expansion of the gross margin, which has risen from 48.8% in 2021 to 55.4% in 2025. This improvement appears to be driven by a successful strategic transition from using high-cost subcontractors to an internalised labour model. While statutory operating profit has shown some volatility, this is largely artificial. The 2025 administrative expenses include £64,000 in director pension contributions and a £32,145 loss on asset disposal; adjusting for these items reveals a much stronger underlying EBITDA. The balance sheet is a significant strength, remaining debt-free with a current ratio of 2.97 even after a period of high dividend extraction. The recent erosion of net assets (down 22.4% in 2025) is entirely due to the timing of dividend payments rather than a decline in trading performance. For an acquirer, the business offers a clean, unleveraged platform with significant 'add-back' potential. The primary due diligence focus should be on the 2026 management accounts to ensure the cost-of-sales trajectory remains in line with the 2025 internalisation strategy."}}	{"balanceSheet":{"id":87,"financialPeriodId":101,"targetCompanyId":26,"intangibleAssets":"1100","tangibleFixedAssets":"55326","freeholdProperty":null,"leaseholdProperty":null,"plantMachinery":"16566","fixturesFittings":"241","motorVehicles":"38519","computerEquipment":null,"stockInventory":null,"tradeDebtors":"4