warranties_indemnities":{"specific_indemnities":["Standard tax and title indemnities.","Exclusion of CMJ Global loan from the locked box equity value."],"warranty_cap_percent":25,"warranty_period_months":12,"de_minimis":10000,"basket":25000},"completion_mechanism":{"type":"locked_box","working_capital_peg":null,"rationale":"Provides price certainty to the seller, assuming a recent balance sheet date (e.g., May 2025)."},"effective_price":{"best_case":750000,"expected_case":712500,"worst_case":600000}}],"recommended_structure":"Conservative","recommendation_rationale":"The combination of 42% customer concentration and a £180k intercompany loan (representing ~35% of the equity value) makes an upfront-heavy deal too risky. The Conservative structure protects the buyer against the 'single point of failure' risk of Knowledge Properties Ltd while ensuring the seller is only paid for the intercompany loan if it is actually recovered.","negotiation_priorities":["Treatment of the £180k CMJ Global loan as a debt-like item (deduction from EV).","Verification of the 2026 'Correction' entries in management accounts.","Securing a multi-year earnout tied specifically to the top 2 clients.","Normalizing the MD salary to £57.5k in the EBITDA calculation."],"deal_breaker_terms":["Refusal to provide a specific indemnity for the £180k intercompany loan.","Evidence of Knowledge Properties Ltd intending to tender their contract elsewhere.","Discovery that the 2026 'Corrections' relate to systemic revenue overstatement in 2025."],"key_findings":["The business is highly efficient but fragile due to extreme client concentration.","The £180k intercompany loan is a major hurdle for a clean 'Cash-Free Debt-Free' deal.","Reported EBITDA is inflated by ~25% due to owner salary structures.","The aging vehicle fleet represents a hidden future liability (Capex Cliff)."],"narrative":"Surrey Ceiling is a high-margin, high-growth business that is currently 'dressed for sale' with low owner salaries and