test_interest_coverage":70.8,"interest_coverage_trend":"improving","additional_debt_capacity_assessment":"Exceptional. The business has negligible gearing and high interest coverage, suggesting significant capacity for acquisition debt."},"key_findings":["Revenue has demonstrated a strong 20% CAGR over the 4-year period, accelerating significantly in 2025.","Gross margins have stabilised in the 57% range after initial compression from 2021 highs.","Operating leverage is evident in 2025, where a 34.6% revenue increase led to a 60.7% increase in operating profit.","The company maintains a robust cash position (£259k in 2025) despite aggressive dividend payouts.","Staff costs are the primary overhead driver, but remain well-controlled relative to revenue growth.","The wholesale division (20% of revenue) provides a higher-margin, lower-labour diversification stream.","Interest coverage is exceptionally high (70.8x), indicating a very low-risk financial profile."],"red_flags":["Aggressive dividend policy: 2024 dividends (£134k) exceeded net profit (£104k), leading to net asset contraction.","Significant 'Corrections' entry in 2026 management accounts requires forensic verification as it materially inflates profit.","Concentration risk in trade debtors, with one client representing over 40% of the ledger in late 2025."],"narrative":"Surrey Ceiling presents a strong financial profile characterised by robust organic growth and high operational efficiency. Revenue has grown from £384k in 2021 to nearly £800k in 2025, representing a 20% CAGR. While gross margins compressed between 2021 and 2023, they have since stabilised at approximately 57%, suggesting the business has successfully navigated inflationary pressures in the construction sector. The 2025 performance is particularly noteworthy, showing significant margin expansion as the business scaled, with net margins reaching 21.9%.\\n\\nThe cost structure is well-managed, with administrative expenses growing at a slower rate than gross profit. Staff costs are the largest overhead component, which is typical for the sector, but the growing wholesale division provides a useful hedge against labour-intensive installation work. Earnings quality is generally high, with operating profit translating consistently into net income, although the 2026 management accounts contain a 'corrections' line that must be scrutinised during full due diligence.\\n\\nFrom a balance sheet perspective, the company is highly liquid and virtually debt-free, with an interest coverage ratio of 70.8x. The primary concern for a buyer is the aggressive extraction of capital; dividends have frequently matched or exceeded annual profits, which resulted in a decline in net assets between 2022 and 2024. However, the 2025 recovery in retained earnings suggests the business can support this level of extraction while maintaining a healthy cash buffer. Overall, the financial trajectory is accelerating, making it an attractive acquisition target with substantial capacity for leverage if required."}}	{"balanceSheet":{"id":73,"financialPeriodId":87,"targetCompanyId":22,"intangibleAssets":null,"tangibleFixedAssets":null,"freeholdProperty":null,"leaseholdProperty":null,"plantMachinery":null,"fixturesFittings":null,"motorVehicles":null,"computerEquipment":null,"stockInventory":null,"tradeDebtors":null,"prepayments":null,"amountsOwedByDirectors":null,"amountsOwedByConnectedCompanies":null,"directorsCurrentAccount":null,"otherDebtors":null,"cashAtBank":null,"totalCurrentAssets":null,"tradeCreditors":null,"taxationSocialSecurity":null,"vatPayable":null,"corporationTaxDue":null,"bankLoansOverdraftCurrent":null,"hirePurchaseCurrent":null,"accruals":null,"amountsOwedToDirectors":null,"otherCreditors":null,"totalCurrentLiabilities":null,"bankLoansLongTerm":null,"hirePurchaseLongTerm":null,"deferredTaxation":null,"otherLongTermDebt":null,"totalLongTermLiabilities":null,"shareCapital":null,"retainedEarnings":null,"totalEquity":null,"debtor