op","Gross margins do not compress further below the 26% floor","The £500k intercompany receivable is collectible and does not impair liquidity"],"sensitivity_factors":["Direct labour wage inflation","Telecoms contract renewal terms","Maintenance requirements of aging plant"],"upside_scenarios":["Utilization of the £1.33m freehold property to secure cheaper growth capital","Professionalization of credit control to reduce the cash conversion cycle"],"downside_scenarios":["Continued margin erosion due to inability to pass on material costs","Loss of a major Telecoms client (high concentration risk)"]},"key_findings":["Reported 2026 EBITDA is heavily distorted by a £69k tax credit and £21k bad debt recovery.","Underlying trading is under pressure, with a 9.2% revenue decline and significant gross margin compression (from 33% to 26%).","The business is 'over-maintaining' or 'under-investing' in cycles, with R&M costs fluctuating wildly.","Director extraction via pensions is highly volatile and provides a significant normalization opportunity."],"red_flags":["31.6% revenue collapse in the Panels division in the most recent year.","Debtor days increasing significantly despite falling revenue.","£500k intercompany loan to a connected entity with no stated repayment terms."],"narrative":"DNA presents a complex QoE profile. While the 'Base Case' Adjusted EBITDA of £624k is higher than the reported figure due to excessive pension contributions, the quality of the underlying revenue is deteriorating. The 9.2% revenue drop and the 660bps margin compression since 2024 suggest the business is losing competitive positioning or failing to manage inflationary pressures. The 2026 'Net Profit' is an accounting fiction supported by a tax credit. A buyer should value the business on the sustainable £624k EBITDA but apply a lower multiple to reflect the negative growth trajectory and segment-specific risks in the Panels division."},"valuation":{"expert":"valuation","company_name":"DNA","