ent MD is non-working. While a GM is in place at £75k, a business of this scale (£3.5m+ revenue) requires a dedicated MD/CEO. Benchmark for £1m-£5m revenue is £45k-£70k; mid-point £57.5k applied.","confidence":"medium","defensibility_score":6,"case_for_inclusion":"A professional buyer cannot rely solely on a General Manager; a replacement for the owner's statutory and strategic duties is required at market rates.","case_for_exclusion":"The seller will argue the existing General Manager at £75k already fulfills the leadership role, making an additional MD salary a double-count."},{"item":"Normalized Maintenance Capex / Depreciation","category":"deferred_maintenance","amount":96582,"recurrence":"recurring","year":2025,"justification":"The company recently moved from a zero-asset model to owning £415k in plant. The 2025 depreciation charge (£10k) is artificially low due to mid-year timing. A normalized 25% reducing balance on £426k gross assets suggests a £106.6k annual charge; deduction is the delta.","confidence":"medium","defensibility_score":7,"case_for_inclusion":"The current depreciation charge does not reflect the true long-term cost of maintaining and replacing the newly acquired specialized fleet.","case_for_exclusion":"Depreciation is a non-cash item and should be excluded from EBITDA by definition; maintenance is already captured in 'Repairs & Maintenance' overheads."}],"total_add_backs":84066,"total_deductions":154082,"adjusted_ebitda":{"conservative":764484,"base":848550,"optimistic":1002632},"adjustment_summary":{"reported_ebitda":918566,"plus_add_backs":84066,"minus_deductions":154082,"equals_adjusted_ebitda_base":848550},"director_salary_analysis":{"directors":[{"name":"AF","current_salary":21666,"estimated_market_rate":0,"benchmark_range_used":"Non-working/sleeping director: £0","adjustment":21666,"adjustment_type":"add_back"}]},"sustainability_assessment":{"sustainable_for_3_years":true,"key_assumptions":["The shift from subcontractors to internal delivery (owned plant) maintains the current 54.9% gross margin.","The General Manager (MB) remains with the business to provide operational continuity.","Motor expenses, which spiked 68% in 2025, can be stabilized through better fleet management."],"sensitivity_factors":["Fuel price volatility impacting the high-mobility cost base.","Retention of the 28 site-based subcontractors who are not directly employed.","Verification of the 'Zero Trade Debtors' anomaly in 2025 accounts."],"upside_scenarios":["Utilizing the debt-free balance sheet to acquire additional specialized plant and further reduce subcontractor margin leakage.","Filling the current 'Admin Assistant' vacancy to free up the GM for business development."],"downside_scenarios":["Loss of the General Manager (MB) who holds the primary operational knowledge.","A 'Capex Cliff' in 5 years if the current modern fleet is not maintained with a consistent replacement cycle."]},"key_findings":["Reported EBITDA is significantly bolstered by a strategic shift to owned assets, but the P&L does not yet reflect the full annual cost of fleet replacement.","Earnings quality is high but requires normalization for aggressive pension extraction and the lack of a market-rate MD salary.","The business is exceptionally liquid, though the £940k Director Loan Account suggests the owner is effectively the primary lender to the business."],"red_flags":["The 2025 year-end shows £0 Trade Debtors, which is highly improbable for a £3.5m utility business; this suggests a potential 'window-dressing' of cash or a major billing error.","Motor expenses (£280k) are rising significantly faster than revenue, indicating potential inefficiencies in fleet deployment.","Extreme management concentration in one non-director employee (GM)."],"narrative":"TLF Utilities shows a strong upward trend in earnings, with Adjusted EBITDA of £848k (Base Case). The primary adjustments involve adding back discretionary owner costs (pension and non-working salary) while deducting the cost of a replacement MD and normalizing the depreciation/maintenance profile of the new fleet. While the margin expansion from 35% to 55% is impressive, it is predicated on the successful management of owned assets versus subcontractors. A buyer should focus on the 'Zero Debtor' anomaly and the sustainability of the GM's workload, as the business is currently 'top-light' in its management structure."},"valuation":{"expert":"valuation","company_name":"TLF UTILITIES","analysis_date":"2025-05-20","score":85,"confidence":"high","ebitda_multiple_valuation":{"adjusted_ebitda_base":848550,"sector_multiple_range":{"low":2,"mid":2.75,"high":3.5},"selected_multiple":3,"multiple_justification":"A multiple of 3.0x is selected, sitting above the sector midpoint but within the SME guardrails. This reflects exceptional margin expansion (54.9% gross margin) and a highly modern asset base (97% freshness), offset by the company's small scale (<£5m revenue) and high management concentration in a single non-director employee.","premium_factors_applied":["Exceptional gross and operating margin expansion","Modern, specialized asset base (97.4% freshness ratio)","Debt-free balance sheet with high liquidity","Strategic shift to in-house delivery model"],"discount_factors_applied":["Revenue under £5M limits buyer pool","High dependency on a single General Manager (MB)","Project-based revenue rather than long-term contracted recurring revenue","Significant 'Zero Debtor' anomaly in 2025 accounts"],"enterprise_value_range":{"conservative":1911210,"base":2545650,"optimistic":3509212}},"ev_to_equity_bridge":{"enterprise_value_base":2545650,"plus_cash":647000,"minus_long_term_debt":0,"minus_director_loans":940206,"minus_bad_debt_provision":0,"minus_working_capital_adjustment":0,"equity_value":{"conservative":1618004,"base":2252444,"optimistic":3215006}},"asset_based_valuation":{"net_asset_value":2580000,"property_adjustment":0,"bad_debt_adjustment":0,"adjusted_nav":2580000,"na