 the benchmark."},{"item":"Fleet Maintenance & Replacement Provision","category":"deferred_maintenance","amount":11949,"recurrence":"recurring","year":2024,"justification":"Operational expert notes motor vehicle NBV decreased 29% in 12 months and the fleet is mid-lifecycle. Current depreciation (£35,732) is slightly below the estimated maintenance capex requirement of £47,681 (based on 2025 draft depreciation) to keep the fleet fresh.","confidence":"medium","defensibility_score":6,"case_for_inclusion":"The rapid depreciation of the vehicle fleet requires a higher cash-neutral provision to ensure the operational capacity is maintained.","case_for_exclusion":"The seller will argue that depreciation is a non-cash item and current maintenance spend is already captured in 'Motor Expenses'."}],"total_add_backs":12867,"total_deductions":67469,"adjusted_ebitda":{"conservative":371686,"base":384553,"optimistic":397420},"adjustment_summary":{"reported_ebitda":439206,"plus_add_backs":12867,"minus_deductions":67469,"equals_adjusted_ebitda_base":384553},"director_salary_analysis":{"directors":[{"name":"Combined Directors (2)","current_salary":59480,"estimated_market_rate":115000,"benchmark_range_used":"Revenue £5M-£10M: MD (£77.5k) + Part-time FD (£37.5k)","adjustment":55520,"adjustment_type":"deduction"}]},"sustainability_assessment":{"sustainable_for_3_years":true,"key_assumptions":["Subcontractor costs stabilize at ~44% of revenue","Domestic high-net-worth market remains resilient to interest rate pressures","The negative cash conversion cycle continues to fund growth without external debt"],"sensitivity_factors":["Subcontractor labor availability","Material price inflation","Owner-client relationship transferability"],"upside_scenarios":["Tightening subcontractor procurement to recover 2023 margin levels (27%)","Diversification into commercial contracts to reduce domestic concentration"],"downside_scenarios":["Further gross margin compression below 15% due to labor shortages","Loss of key subcontractor relationships post-acquisition"]},"key_findings":["Reported EBITDA is inflated by under-market director salaries, which are offset by high dividend extractions.","The business model is highly scalable but currently showing signs of 'growth at any cost' with significant margin erosion in 2024.","Working capital is a major strength; the negative cash conversion cycle is a rare and valuable attribute in construction.","The 2025 draft figures suggest revenue is plateauing, making the 2024 'peak' revenue a critical point for valuation discipline."],"red_flags":["Gross margin volatility (13.7% to 27.1%) suggests a lack of pricing power or poor project estimating.","High reliance on subcontractors (83% of COS) leaves the buyer with limited control over the primary cost base.","85% revenue concentration in the domestic sector creates niche market risk."],"narrative":"Agincourt Contractors demonstrates a robust but volatile earnings profile. While the reported 2024 EBITDA of £439k is impressive, it is structurally reliant on the owners taking minimal salaries. After adjusting for a professional management team and a realistic fleet maintenance provision, the Base Case Adjusted EBITDA is £384,553. The primary concern for a buyer is the 1,000 bps drop in gross margin during the 2024 expansion. This suggests that as the company scales, it loses efficiency or overpays for external labor. However, the exceptional working capital management—where the business is effectively funded by its suppliers—provides a significant 'hidden' value that supports the sustainability of these earnings even if margins remain at the lower 17% threshold."},"valuation":{"expert":"valuation","company_name":"Agincourt Contractors","analysis_date":"2025-05-23","score":72,"confidence":"high","ebitda_multiple_valuation":{"adjusted_ebitda_base":384553,"sector_multiple_range":{"low":2.5,"mid":3.5,"high":4.5},"selected_multiple":3,"multiple_justification":"A 3.0x multiple is selected, sitting at the lower-mid end of the sector range. While revenue exceeds the £5M threshold, the business suffers from significant margin volatility (1000bps drop in 2024) and high key-man dependency. The subcontractor-led model provides scalability but lacks the 'moat' of a direct-delivery or recurring-service model.","premium_factors_applied":["Revenue exceeds £5M threshold","Negative cash conversion cycle (supply chain funded growth)","Strong liquidity and minimal gearing (3.86%)"],"discount_factors_applied":["Significant gross margin compression (27% to 17%)","High reliance on subcontractors (83% of COS)","85% revenue concentration in domestic sector","Thin management depth (key-man risk)"],"enterprise_value_range":{"conservative":929215,"base":1153659,"optimistic":1538212}},"ev_to_equity_bridge":{"enterprise_value_base":1153659,"plus_cash":184000,"minus_long_term_debt":25000,"minus_director_loans":601,"minus_bad_debt_provision":12867,"minus_working_capital_adjustment":0,"equity_value":{"conservative":1074747,"base":1299191,"optimistic":1683744}},"asset_based_valuation":{"net_asset_value":647438,"property_adjustment":0,"bad_debt_adjustment":-12867,"adjusted_nav":634571,"nav_as_floor":true},"revenue_multiple_crosscheck":{"latest_revenue":5427172,"revenue_multiple_range":{"low":0.3,"high":0.5},"implied_ev_range":{"low":1628151,"high":2713586}},"valuation_summary":{"recommended_range":{"equity_value_low":1075000,"equity_value_mid":1300000,"equity_value_high":1685000},"primary_methodology":"ebitda_multiple","cross_check_alignment":"moderate"},"sensitivity":{"ebitda_plus_10pct":{"enterprise_value":1269025,"equity_value":1414557},"ebitda_minus_10pct":{"enterprise_value":1038293,"equity_value":1183825},"multiple_plus_half":{"enterprise_value":1345936,"equity_value":1491468},"multiple_minus_half":{"enterprise_value":961383,"equity_value":1106915},"key_customer_loss_impact":"High. 85% domestic concentration suggests individual project loss could impact annual EBITDA by 15-20%.","director_replacement_impact":"55520"},"seller_expectations":{"likely_seller_expectation_range":{"low":1600000,"high":2700000},"seller_anchor_points":["2024 Peak Revenue (£5.4M)","Reported EBITDA (£439k) without salary adjustments","Historical 27% Gross Margins"],"negotiation_gap_assessment":"Significant. The seller is likely to anchor on the 2024 revenue peak and reported EBITDA. A buyer will focus on the 2025 plateau and the 1000bps margin drop. The gap is approximately £300k-£500k."},"key_findings":["The business is a clean target with high liquidity and low debt, supporting a smooth equity bridge.","Adjusted EBITDA of £384k is the most reliable earnings base, accounting for necessary management replacement costs.","The negative cash conversion cycle is a standout feature, providing 'hidden' value as the business funds its own growth.","Revenue multiple cross-check suggests a higher value, but in the construction sector, EBITDA multiples are more reflective of risk-adjusted returns."],"red_flags":["Severe margin erosion in 2024 suggests lack of pricing power at scale.","High dividend extraction (£258k) relative to net profit (£294k) indicates a lifestyle-oriented business model.","2025 draft figures show revenue plateauing, suggesting the business may have reached its current operational ceiling."],"narrative":"Agincourt Contractors is a financially sound but operationally volatile business. The valuation is anchored by a strong balance sheet and exceptional working capital management, which justifies a mid-market multiple of 3.0x despite the risks. However, the significant drop in gross margin as the company scaled to £5.4M revenue is a major concern for any buyer; it suggests that the current management struggled to control subcontractor costs during the growth spurt. The fair equity value sits between £1.1M and £1.7M. To bridge the gap with likely higher seller expectations, a deal structure involving an earn-out based on margin recovery (returning to >20% levels) would be highly appropriate."},"deal_structure":{"expert":"deal_structure","company_name":"Agincourt Contractors","analysis_date":"2025-05-24","score":65,"confidence":"high","structures":[{"name":"Conservative","strategy":"Maximum buyer protection focusing on margin recovery and key-man transition.","suitable_when":"High risk profile due to 1000bps margin drop and extreme key-man dependency.","consideration":{"total_headline":1150000,"upfront_cash":575000,"upfront_percent":50,"deferred":230000,"deferred_schedule":"Two equal installments of £115,000 at 12 and 24 months post-completion.","earnout_maximum":345000,"earnout_metric":"Gross Margin % and EBITDA Floor","earnout_period_months":24,"earnout_rationale":"Addresses 'Owner/key person dependency' and 'Sector-specific risk' (margin erosion). The QoE expert identified a 10% drop in gross margin; this earnout requires margins to return to >20% to trigger full payment.","retention_escrow":115000,"escrow_release_conditions":"Released after 18 months pending no warranty claims related to undisclosed subcontractor liabilities."},"seller_obligations":{"handover_period_months":12,"non_compete_months":36,"non_compete_scope":"High-end domestic construction and renovation within a 50-mile radius of current operations.","consultancy_arrangement":"Directors to remain as consultants for months 7-12 at a fixed daily rate to transition HNW client relationships."},"warranties_indemnities":{"specific_indemnities":["Specific indemnity for any CIS (Construction Industry Scheme) tax underpayments.","Indemnity for 'Other Debtors' (£284k) if not fully recoverable within 6 months.","Indemnity for any litigation arising from 2024 subcontractor disputes."],"warranty_cap_percent":50,"warranty_period_months":24,"de_minimis":5000,"basket":25000},"completion_mechanism":{"type":"completion_accounts","working_capital_peg":321465,"rationale":"Volatility in WIP and Trade Debtors requires a true-up at completion to ensure the negative 