when":"Competitive bidding environment where the buyer prioritizes securing the asset over maximum risk mitigation.","consideration":{"total_headline":1500000,"upfront_cash":1200000,"upfront_percent":80,"deferred":150000,"deferred_schedule":"Paid at 6 months post-completion.","earnout_maximum":150000,"earnout_metric":"Revenue Retention","earnout_period_months":12,"earnout_rationale":"Addresses 'Revenue stagnation' risk identified in 2025 draft figures. Earnout triggers if 2025/26 revenue stays above £5M.","retention_escrow":50000,"escrow_release_conditions":"Released after 12 months."},"seller_obligations":{"handover_period_months":3,"non_compete_months":24,"non_compete_scope":"Standard non-solicitation of clients and staff.","consultancy_arrangement":"Ad-hoc consultancy for 6 months."},"warranties_indemnities":{"specific_indemnities":["Standard tax covenant."],"warranty_cap_percent":20,"warranty_period_months":12,"de_minimis":10000,"basket":50000},"completion_mechanism":{"type":"locked_box","working_capital_peg":321465,"rationale":"Simplifies the closing process to appeal to a lifestyle-oriented seller."},"effective_price":{"best_case":1500000,"expected_case":1425000,"worst_case":1200000}}],"recommended_structure":"Conservative","recommendation_rationale":"The 1000bps drop in gross margin while scaling is a massive red flag that suggests the business model may be broken at higher volumes. A Conservative structure with a heavy earnout tied to margin recovery is essential to ensure the buyer isn't overpaying for 'unprofitable growth'. Furthermore, the high key-man dependency in a relationship-driven domestic market necessitates a long handover and deferred payments to ensure the seller is incentivized to transition HNW clients properly.","negotiation_priorities":["Verification of 'Other Debtors' (£284k) which is unusually high compared to Trade Debtors.","Securing a long-term non-compete to prevent the sellers from starting a new boutique firm with the same subcontractors.","Establishing a clear 'Gross Margin' definition for earnout purposes to prevent accounting manipulation.","Ensuring the negative cash conversion cycle is protected through a robust working capital peg."],"deal_breaker_terms":["Refusal of a margin-based earnout given the 2024 performance.","Discovery that 'Other Debtors' are actually unrecoverable director-related balances.","Inability of the seller to provide a clean 2-year non-compete."],"key_findings":["The business is financially 'clean' (low debt, high liquidity) but operationally fragile due to margin volatility.","The negative cash conversion cycle is the most valuable intangible asset and must be protected in the deal structure.","The gap between the valuation (£1.3M) and seller expectations (up to £2.7M) is too wide for a cash-upfront deal; an earnout is the only bridge.","Subcontractor-led models are easy to scale but hard to defend; the deal must focus on the 'management layer' value."],"narrative":"Agincourt Contractors is a classic 'lifestyle' business that has grown beyond its management's ability to control costs. While the balance sheet is excellent, the 2024 P&L shows a company struggling with its own success. The recommended Conservative structure protects the buyer from the 'growth at any cost' mentality seen in the recent figures. By tying 30% of the consideration to margin recovery and relationship retention, the buyer mitigates the risk that they are buying a business at its peak with declining profitability. The use of completion accounts is vital here to ensure that the high WIP and debtor balances are accurately valued at the point of transfer."},"financial":{"expert":"financial","company_name":"Agincourt Contractors","analysis_date":"2025-05-20","score":68,"confidence":"high","yearly_metrics":{"2021":{"revenue":4058518,"revenue_yoy_growth_percent":null,"cost_of_sales":3255587,"gross_profit":802931,"gross_margin_percent":19.78,"administrative_expenses":498338,"operating_profit":309960