,"The business maintains a lean fixed-asset base, with motor expenses being the largest single overhead category.","Profitability peaked in 2023; subsequent years show margin compression despite higher revenue levels.","Liquidity is strong and improving, with a current ratio of 1.53 and a significant recovery in cash reserves to £184k."],"red_flags":["Significant gross margin compression from 27.1% (2023) to 16.9% (2024).","High reliance on subcontractor labour (44% of total revenue in 2024), creating exposure to external inflationary pressures.","Substantial dividend payments (£258k in 2024) relative to net profit (£294k) may limit internal reinvestment capacity."],"narrative":"Agincourt Contractors presents a profile of a scalable but margin-sensitive construction business. The financial data reveals a company that successfully navigated a significant growth spurt in 2024, with revenue increasing by nearly 32%. However, this growth came at the expense of operational efficiency; gross margins compressed by over 1000 basis points in the same period, primarily due to a 46.7% surge in subcontractor costs. This suggests that while the company can win larger contracts, its ability to maintain price discipline or manage external labour costs diminishes at scale. \\n\\nFrom an earnings quality perspective, the results are generally positive. Operating profit tracks closely with EBITDA, and the overhead base is remarkably lean and predominantly variable, providing a safety net during downturns. The 2025 draft figures indicate a slight cooling of the market, with revenue plateauing, yet margins appear to be stabilising around the 17-18% mark. \\n\\nThe balance sheet is a point of significant strength. The company has successfully deleveraged, clearing bank overdrafts and reducing hire purchase obligations, resulting in a very conservative gearing ratio of 3.86%. With an interest coverage ratio exceeding 120x, the business is ideally positioned to take on acquisition debt. F