ividend extraction has historically weakened the capital base (Net Assets fell 33.8% in 2025).","Lack of 2026 Balance Sheet data prevents verification of current liquidity or year-end cash position."],"narrative":"DHA Fire presents a financial profile characterized by strong top-line momentum and a recent, significant recovery in profitability. Between 2023 and 2025, the company faced severe margin pressure, with net margins collapsing from 14.4% to just 1.2% as cost of sales outpaced revenue growth. However, the 2026 management accounts indicate a successful turnaround or a shift in project mix, with gross margins rebounding to 49.5% and operating profit reaching £515k. This volatility suggests that while the business is capable of high performance, it is sensitive to direct cost inputs or pricing strategy. From an M&A perspective, the company is attractive due to its debt-free status and lean fixed-cost base, which is predominantly variable through the use of subcontractors. The primary concern for a buyer is the historical dividend policy; the directors have consistently extracted more than the annual net profit, which has eroded the net asset position. Future acquisition debt could be comfortably serviced by the 2026 earnings level, but a buyer must ensure that the recent margin expansion is sustainable and not the result of a single high-margin contract. The high travel and subsistence spend (£74k-£98k) suggests an opportunity for operational tightening post-acquisition."}}	{"balanceSheet":{"id":300,"financialPeriodId":363,"targetCompanyId":74,"intangibleAssets":null,"tangibleFixedAssets":null,"freeholdProperty":null,"leaseholdProperty":null,"plantMachinery":null,"fixturesFittings":null,"motorVehicles":null,"computerEquipment":null,"stockInventory":null,"tradeDebtors":null,"prepayments":null,"amountsOwedByDirectors":null,"amountsOwedByConnectedCompanies":null,"directorsCurrentAccount":null,"otherDebtors":null,"cashAtBank":null,"totalCurrentAssets":null,"tradeCr