 was due to the directors 'switching off' and that the client relationships were too personal to transfer.","The 'clean' balance sheet will be found to have significant contingent liabilities related to historical customs declarations."]},"investment_thesis":{"positive_case":"This is a 'buy-the-balance-sheet' turnaround. By acquiring a debt-free entity with £360k cash for a price near its Net Asset Value, the buyer gains a platform with high-margin customs expertise. Value is created by immediately cutting £150k+ in director costs and rationalizing the £711k overheads to match the £1.6m revenue, returning the business to a 10-15% EBITDA margin.","conditions_for_success":["Revenue must stabilize at or above £1.5m during the first 12 months.","The buyer must successfully transition key client relationships away from the departing directors.","The overhead base must be reduced by at least £200k within the first 100 days."],"integration_priorities_100_days":["Immediate termination of current director remuneration and pension structures.","Full audit of the sales ledger to identify and 'ring-fence' the top 5 clients.","Implementation of a new financial reporting suite to replace the current owner-managed system.","Fleet/Asset health check and procurement of 1-2 replacement vehicles if required."]},"information_requests":{"critical":[{"request":"Aged Sales Ledger and Customer Concentration Report","source_expert":"Risk","rationale":"To determine if the revenue decline is due to one major loss or a general trend, and to assess the risk of further attrition."},{"request":"Detailed breakdown of 'Administrative Expenses' (£711k)","source_expert":"Financial","rationale":"To identify specific areas for immediate cost-cutting post-acquisition."}],"important":[{"request":"Asset Register with service history and age of vehicles/equipment","source_expert":"Operational","rationale":"To quantify the 'Capex Cliff' and estimate immediate reinvestment needs."},{"request":"Confirmation of 'No Leakage' since 2025 year-end","source_expert":"Deal Structure","rationale":"To ensure the £360k cash hasn't been extracted via dividends or bonuses prior to the deal."}],"nice_to_have":[{"request":"Details of any specialist customs software or AEO certifications","source_expert":"Sector","rationale":"To assess the 'moat' and scalability of the customs brokerage arm."}]},"valuation_summary":{"equity_value_range":{"low":345927,"mid":425000,"high":508888},"recommended_offer":345927,"recommended_structure":"Conservative: £150k upfront, £96k deferred over 24 months, £100k earnout based on revenue stabilization."},"narrative_report":"INVESTMENT MEMORANDUM: NORTHERN INTERNATIONAL EXPORT LIMITED\\n\\n1. STRATEGIC OVERVIEW\\nNorthern International Export Limited (NIEL) is a micro-SME operating in the logistics and customs brokerage sector. While the sector has benefited from post-Brexit complexity, NIEL appears to be in a state of managed decline. Revenue has contracted from £2.62m in 2022 to £1.61m in 2025. However, the company has maintained an exceptionally clean balance sheet, characterized by zero debt and a cash balance of £360,927. The investment opportunity lies not in the current earnings—which are negative—but in the potential to restructure a liquid, high-margin service provider.\\n\\n2. FINANCIAL PERFORMANCE & QUALITY OF EARNINGS\\nThe financial trajectory is concerning. Revenue fell by 22.6% in the last year alone. More critically, the company exhibits severe negative operating leverage; administrative expenses have remained at c.£711k despite a £1m drop in turnover. This has resulted in a 2025 operating loss of £88,775. Quality of Earnings (QoE) analysis reveals an adjusted EBITDA of -£78,342. While we can 'add back' excessive director remuneration of £65k, we must also 'deduct' for a lack of professional management and deferred maintenance. The business is currently a 'melting ice cube' that will exhaust its cash reserves within 3-4 years if the current trend continues.\\n\\n3. OPERATIONAL VIABILITY & RISK\\nOperationally, the business is stable but under-invested. Asset freshness is low (27.5%), indicating a looming 'capex cliff' where the fleet or equipment will require urgent replacement. The business is also highly owner-dependent, with the two directors accounting for 35% of the total payroll. The primary risk is 'Revenue Attrition'; the 39% decline over three years suggests a loss of market relevance or the departure of key accounts. Without a clear understanding of the customer base, the risk of further collapse post-acquisition is high.\\n\\n4. VALUATION & DEAL STRUCTURE\\nTraditional earnings-based valuation (EBITDA multiples) is inapplicable given the negative adjusted earnings. Consequently, we have adopted an asset-based valuation approach, using the Net Asset Value (NAV) as a floor. We recommend a headline equity value of £345,927. To protect the buyer, the deal must be structured defensively: an upfront payment of £150k (less than the cash on hand), with the remainder tied to a 24-month deferred schedule and a revenue-based earnout. This ensures the sellers remain incentivized to stabilize the revenue floor.\\n\\n5. INVESTMENT THESIS & CONCLUSION\\nThe case for acquisition rests on the 'Turnaround' potential. NIEL has a high gross margin (38.7%), suggesting its core customs and export services are valued by the market. If a buyer can immediately right-size the overheads—specifically by replacing the expensive directors with a leaner management structure—the business can be returned to profitability. The £360k cash provides the 'runway' to execute this transition. However, the deal is only recommended if the buyer has the operational expertise to manage a logistics turnaround and can verify the stability of the remaining client base during due diligence."}	\N	\N	\N	17088	{"promptTokenCount":11096,"candidatesTokenCount":2664,"totalTokenCount":13760}	15	{financial,operational,risk,qoe,sector,valuation,de