cent":15.35,"gross_profit_cagr_percent":15.01,"operating_profit_cagr_percent":null,"period_years":3},"cost_structure":{"cost_of_sales_percent_revenue":80.31,"overheads_percent_revenue":16.9,"staff_costs_percent_revenue":36.36,"director_costs_percent_revenue":8.12,"top_5_overheads":[{"category":"Directors Salaries","value":163116,"percent_of_overheads":42.69},{"category":"Rent & Rates","value":53227,"percent_of_overheads":13.93},{"category":"Advertising & Marketing","value":28352,"percent_of_overheads":7.42},{"category":"IT Support & Hosting","value":26039,"percent_of_overheads":6.82},{"category":"Employers NI","value":18743,"percent_of_overheads":4.91}],"cost_base_classification":"predominantly_variable"},"earnings_quality":{"net_profit_tracks_operating_profit":true,"exceptional_items_identified":["Furlough receipts 2021","Exceptional project costs 2026","Write-offs 2025"],"tax_rate_consistent":false,"ebitda_latest":67750,"ebitda_trend":"growing","limitations":["Inconsistency between statutory and management reporting gross margins","Depreciation not consistently broken out in all source documents"]},"debt_service":{"latest_interest_coverage":12.5,"interest_coverage_trend":"stable","additional_debt_capacity_assessment":"Moderate capacity based on coverage ratios, but thin net margins limit absolute cash flow available for principal repayment."},"key_findings":["Revenue has grown consistently from £1.47m to £2.26m, representing a healthy 15.35% CAGR.","The business successfully transitioned from an operating loss in 2022 to stable profitability in 2024-2025.","Gross margins are thin for the sector, consistently hovering around the 19-20% mark in statutory filings.","Staff and subcontractor costs are the primary drivers of the cost base, representing over 50% of revenue.","Dividend payments in 2025 (£55k) exceeded net profit (£41.8k), leading to an erosion of the equity base.","Liquidity is tightening, with the quick ratio falling below 1.0 in the latest period, indicating working capital pressure.","Management accounts show significantly higher gross margins (33%) than statutory accounts (20%), suggesting a classification difference in direct vs indirect labour.","Interest coverage remains robust at 12.5x, providing some comfort for debt servicing."],"red_flags":["Dividends consistently exceeding net earnings, eroding shareholder funds.","Quick ratio below 1.0 in 2025, indicating potential difficulty meeting immediate liabilities.","Significant discrepancy between statutory and management reporting of Gross Profit margins.","High reliance on a few key B2B clients (e.g., Savills, LCP) with noted risks of demand drops from LCP."],"narrative":"HPC has demonstrated a positive trajectory in terms of scale, successfully growing revenue by over 50% since 2022 and moving from a loss-making position to a stable, albeit thin, profit margin. The core strength of the business lies in its consistent top-line growth and its established presence in the Heating & Plumbing sector, which provides a reliable recurring revenue stream. However, from a Senior Financial Analyst's perspective, several areas require caution. The statutory gross margins of ~20% are significantly lower than industry benchmarks for property maintenance, suggesting either high pricing pressure or an inefficient use of direct labour and subcontractors. While management accounts suggest a higher margin of 33%, the discrepancy likely stems from the classification of staff costs, which needs thorough reconciliation during formal due diligence. Earnings quality is moderate; while operating profit is stable, the net margin remains below 3%, leaving the company vulnerable to even minor inflationary shocks or increases in overheads. The most significant concern is the aggressive dividend policy. Paying out £55,000 in 2025 against a net profit of £41,870 has directly led to a 15% reduction in net assets and a tightening of liquidity. The quick ratio falling below 1.0 is a clear signal that the business is increasingly reliant on the timely collection of trade debtors to fund its daily operations. For an acquirer, the business offers a solid platform for growth, but the current capital withdrawal pattern is unsustainable. Post-acquisition, a buyer would need to focus on margin expansion through better labour utilisation and a more conservative approach to cash extraction to rebuild the balance sheet's resilience."}	\N	58	high	13620	{"promptTokenCount":56909,"candidatesTokenCount":2407,"totalTokenCount":59316}	21	\N	2026-04-01 12:18:54.529825	2026-04-01 12:19:08.672
