is a process issue, not a credit quality issue."],"sensitivity_factors":["Direct labour costs (currently 23% of revenue) are highly sensitive to minimum wage and freelance rate hikes.","Event sector seasonality and reliance on discretionary corporate spending."],"upside_scenarios":["Professionalisation of debtor collection could release £300k+ in cash.","Transitioning freelance staff to permanent roles could improve gross margins if volume is consistent."],"downside_scenarios":["If intercompany balances are unrecoverable, the balance sheet surplus evaporates.","Stagnant revenue (2022-2023) suggests the business may have hit a ceiling without significant new Capex."]},"key_findings":["Reported 2025 EBITDA is significantly higher than historical levels, requiring verification of the lower cost base.","Earnings quality is diluted by a massive spike in debtor days and high intercompany exposure.","The business has successfully deleveraged, but the 'cash' on the balance sheet is offset by high trade payables (111 days)."],"red_flags":["Debtor days increased by >400% in 3 years.","Intercompany receivables (£504k) represent 27% of current assets.","Creditor days of 111 suggest the business is funding itself by stretching suppliers."],"narrative":"While Bubble shows a strong 2025 recovery on paper, the 'Quality of Earnings' is moderate. The jump in net margin from 0.4% in 2023 to 15.8% in 2025 is aggressive and requires a deep-dive audit to ensure costs haven't been capitalised or deferred. Furthermore, the cash flow profile is concerning; profit is being booked but not collected, as evidenced by the ballooning debtor days. The base case Adjusted EBITDA of £556k reflects a necessary normalisation for director market rates and a prudent provision for the deteriorating aging of the ledger. A buyer should focus on the recoverability of the £504k intercompany loan, as this is the primary risk to the net asset value."}	\N	52	medium	12636	{"promptTokenCount":26799,"candidatesToke