% of total overheads when including NI and pensions.","Liquidity has improved significantly, with cash at bank reaching £778k in 2025, providing a solid buffer for operations.","Intercompany exposure is high, with over £504k owed by connected companies in 2025, representing 27% of current assets."],"red_flags":["Historical technical insolvency and reliance on director support for going concern status (2021-2023).","Significant margin compression observed in 2023 where net profit fell to just 0.4%.","High intercompany receivables (£504k) which may impact actual cash availability if the connected entity is distressed.","Inconsistent effective tax rates suggest potential prior-year adjustments or R&D tax credit volatility."],"narrative":"Bubble presents a financial profile typical of a scaling F&B business that has navigated a significant post-pandemic recovery. The most striking feature is the 2022 turnaround, where revenue nearly tripled and the company moved from a deep loss to profitability. However, the 2023 results serve as a cautionary note; despite maintaining revenue levels, gross margin compression and rising administrative costs nearly wiped out the bottom line, with net margins collapsing to 0.4%. This suggests the business is highly sensitive to cost inflation and may lack the pricing power to pass on rapid increases in COGS. The 2025 data shows a much healthier 15.8% net margin on lower revenue, which may indicate a strategic shift toward higher-margin contracts or better cost management. From an M&A perspective, the balance sheet has de-risked significantly, moving from negative equity to a £623k surplus and eliminating long-term bank debt. The primary financial risk for a buyer lies in the volatility of the earnings stream and the high concentration of value in intercompany receivables. The acquisition decision should hinge on verifying the sustainability of the 2025 margin improvements and ensuring the intercompany balances are recoverable or can be of